# Pipelines

A pipeline changes records on their way in. It reads from a stream, passes each
record through nodes that transform or filter it, and writes the result to
another stream or forwards it somewhere outside OpenObserve.

Three resources are involved, and they only make sense together:

| Resource | What it is |
|---|---|
| `openobserve_pipeline` | The graph: what happens, in what order |
| `openobserve_function` | A transform, written in VRL or JavaScript |
| `openobserve_pipeline_destination` | Somewhere outside OpenObserve to send records |

## The rule that matters most

**Reference the function and the destination. Do not name them.**

```hcl
node {
  id            = "redact"
  type          = "function"
  function_name = openobserve_function.redact_email.name   # not "redact_email"
}
```

The server refuses to delete a function or a destination while a pipeline still
uses it:

```
409  Warning: Function 'redact_email' has 1 pipeline dependencies.
     Please remove these pipelines first: [{"id":"749786...","name":"redact_pii"}]

409  Destination is currently used by pipeline: ship_audit
```

That reference is what tells Terraform to create the function before the
pipeline and to destroy the pipeline before the function. Written as a literal
string, Terraform sees no dependency, and the destroy fails.

## A first pipeline

```hcl
resource "openobserve_function" "redact_email" {
  name = "redact_email"

  function = <<-VRL
    .email = "[redacted]"
  VRL
}

resource "openobserve_pipeline" "redact_pii" {
  name        = "redact_pii"
  description = "Strip email addresses before they land"
  stream_name = openobserve_stream.app_logs.name

  node {
    id          = "in"
    type        = "stream"
    stream_name = openobserve_stream.app_logs.name
  }

  node {
    id            = "redact"
    type          = "function"
    function_name = openobserve_function.redact_email.name
  }

  node {
    id          = "out"
    type        = "stream"
    stream_name = openobserve_stream.app_logs_clean.name
  }

  edge {
    from = "in"
    to   = "redact"
  }

  edge {
    from = "redact"
    to   = "out"
  }
}
```

~> **Edges are multi-line blocks.** HCL allows only one argument on a single
line, so `edge { from = "in", to = "out" }` is a syntax error.

## Node types

| `type` | Needs | Does |
|---|---|---|
| `stream` | `stream_name` | Reads from or writes to a stream |
| `function` | `function_name` | Applies a transform |
| `condition` | `conditions` | Drops records that do not match |
| `remote_stream` | `destination_name` | Forwards to a pipeline destination |

A node block is flat, with a `type` telling the others which fields apply,
rather than a separate block shape per kind. That keeps `dynamic "node"` usable
for a module that builds its graph from a variable.

## What you do not have to write

Three things the API requires and the provider fills in:

**`io_type`** is inferred from the edges. A node with nothing arriving is the
`input`, one with nothing leaving is an `output`, anything else is `default`.

**Node positions** are laid out left to right in declaration order. They only
affect the visual editor, but the server rejects a node without them.

**Edge ids** follow the server's own `e{from}-{to}` convention, so a pipeline
written here looks like one built in the UI.

All three can still be set explicitly when a particular layout matters.

## Functions

The server compiles the body when it is saved, so a mistake fails
`terraform apply` rather than turning up later as a pipeline that quietly
stopped working.

```hcl
resource "openobserve_function" "enrich" {
  name = "enrich_region"

  function = <<-VRL
    .region = if .country == "US" { "amer" } else { "intl" }
  VRL
}
```

VRL is the default. Set `language = "js"` for JavaScript, and the server
compiles it as JavaScript instead.

~> A VRL program's value is its last expression, and a transform has to return
the record, so the server appends a trailing `.` to a body that does not already
end in one. The provider treats that as equivalent to what you wrote, so it does
not appear as drift.

`params` names what the body receives, and defaults to `row`: the whole record,
which is what a pipeline transform wants.

## Filtering with a condition node

`conditions` is a JSON document, so build it with `jsonencode()`. `and`, `or`
and `not` nest, and the leaves are column comparisons. This is the same shape an
alert's `custom` conditions take.

```hcl
node {
  id   = "errors_only"
  type = "condition"

  conditions = jsonencode({
    and = [
      { column = "level", operator = "=", value = "error", ignore_case = false },
    ]
  })
}
```

An empty condition set is rejected: a filter that filters nothing is almost
always a mistake.

## Sending records elsewhere

A `remote_stream` node forwards to a pipeline destination.

```hcl
resource "openobserve_pipeline_destination" "warehouse" {
  name = "warehouse"
  url  = "https://warehouse.example.com/ingest"

  headers = {
    Authorization = "Bearer ${var.warehouse_token}"
  }
}

resource "openobserve_pipeline" "ship_audit" {
  name        = "ship_audit"
  stream_name = openobserve_stream.audit.name

  node {
    id          = "in"
    type        = "stream"
    stream_name = openobserve_stream.audit.name
  }

  node {
    id               = "out"
    type             = "remote_stream"
    destination_name = openobserve_pipeline_destination.warehouse.name
  }

  edge {
    from = "in"
    to   = "out"
  }
}
```

### Pipeline destinations and alert destinations

These are the same object underneath, stored through the same endpoints. The
server tells them apart by one field: a destination carrying a `template` is an
alert destination, and one without is a pipeline destination that alerts cannot
use.

They are separate resources here because that single field decides what the
object is for, and because the rest of the alert surface, templates, email
recipients and SNS, has no meaning for a pipeline. Use
`openobserve_alert_destination` for alerts and `openobserve_pipeline_destination`
for pipelines, and neither can be mistaken for the other.

Set `destination_type` when the far end is a system OpenObserve knows how to
format for: `openobserve`, `splunk`, `elasticsearch`, or `custom`. Leave it
unset for a plain webhook.

~> The URL is resolved when the destination is saved. A host that does not
resolve is rejected with *Destination URL blocked by SSRF guard*, so the
endpoint has to exist by the time Terraform applies.

## One realtime pipeline per source stream

A stream can be the source of only one realtime pipeline:

> `A realtime pipeline with same source stream already exists`

Extend the existing pipeline rather than adding a second one on the same stream.
A pipeline can fan out to several outputs, so this is rarely a real constraint.

## Pausing

`enabled = false` leaves the pipeline configured but idle. Pausing is its own
endpoint rather than a field on the update body, and the provider issues the
extra call when the value changes, so from configuration it simply works.

## Names are lowercased

The server trims a pipeline name and lowercases it, so `My Pipeline` is stored
as `my pipeline`. Writing names lowercase avoids the surprise. Stream names get
similar treatment; see the `openobserve_stream` documentation for
`effective_name`.

## What the provider checks before applying

- Node ids are unique, since edges join nodes by id
- Every edge endpoint names a declared node
- Each node type carries the field it needs
- At least two nodes, and at least one edge

The server additionally checks that the graph is connected and that every node
is reachable from the input.

## Finding what to import

A pipeline's identifier is assigned by the server and appears nowhere in
configuration:

```hcl
data "openobserve_pipelines" "all" {}

output "pipeline_ids" {
  value = { for p in data.openobserve_pipelines.all.pipelines : p.name => p.pipeline_id }
}
```

```bash
terraform import openobserve_pipeline.redact_pii          default/7497861055431835648
terraform import openobserve_function.redact_email        default/redact_email
terraform import openobserve_pipeline_destination.warehouse default/warehouse
```

When a delete is refused and the pipeline holding a function was not created
here, the function data source says which one it is:

```hcl
data "openobserve_function" "redact" {
  name = "redact_email"
}

output "blocking_pipelines" {
  value = data.openobserve_function.redact.used_by
}
```

